// The boss journal: Grubnak, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).grubnak = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Grubnak. An ogre with a cleaver in each hand and a kitchen floor slick with grease. Bad footing everywhere. He never left the floor and never let up. We will be back.",
    gloomsworn: "The Cook. A kitchen that burns, a floor that slides, and cleavers meant for whoever stands in front. I am not afraid of dying in it, I have done that. I am afraid of the footing. The watch goes back in tomorrow.",
    thresher: "Grubnak keeps his kitchen the way some keep a smallholding, everything coming due at once and nothing left standing long. Greasy ground, hot weather, a cleaver for whoever works the front row. We weren't ripe for him yet. Plenty of time.",
    bloodreaver: "The big cook hits like a falling side of beef and never stops serving. Grease, fire, cleavers, all of it in my face. I like him already. Tomorrow I bleed for him properly.",
    runeguard: "The Chef reads plainly enough once one knows his tools; every cleaver, ladle and lid announces itself before it falls. Tonight I recorded the omens. Tomorrow I shall have the answers in order.",
    gorgemaw: "At last, a cook who serves without pause. My host's menu is long and his seasoning heavy-handed; the grease is a disgrace. I sampled a little of everything and lost my seat. Still hungry.",
    oathbroker: "I have reviewed the Ogre Chef's terms: a long service, every course payable in full, no clause for rest. My co-signatories defaulted early. The fine print will be read properly tomorrow. So noted.",
    ironpalm: "The cook speaks quickly and never pauses for an answer. Cleaver, ladle, lid, fire. We heard only half of it tonight. Next time we listen. Breathe.",
    ranger: "Ogre in a long kitchen. Greasy floor, casks for cover. Never stops cooking. Need better lanes.",
    shadowblade: "The mark is an ogre in an apron who waves his cleavers at whoever stands before him. Charming. He turns round far too often, but he has a back, and I found it.",
    pyromancer: "Grubnak cooks with fire, and badly! The whole kitchen smoked and spat, and I spent more time running than casting. My fringe will never forgive him.",
    thermist: "Hot kitchen, ogre, cleavers. I ran cold reading it. Then it all came at once, grease and fire and plates, and I was scalding before I knew it. Too far. Back tomorrow.",
    driftblade: "The cook never stops and neither did I, through the grease and round the casks and out past his cleavers, and it was glorious right up until the floor caught fire under me.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man is an ogre in an apron, and the stage is a kitchen. Grease on the boards, fire in the wings. The company missed half its cues. (Exit, pursued by a cleaver.)",
    cleric: "The ogre feeds his kitchen with heroes, and he very nearly fed it us. Every course reached all of us, and the grease underfoot made everyone careless. Dawn keep us tomorrow.",
    grovekeeper: "The old ogre's kitchen is all weather: grease falls like rain, fire rolls in like a dry summer, and nothing stops for breath. I'll learn its seasons. My lot will need roots.",
    chronomancer: "Grubnak runs his kitchen in courses, each faster than the last, the same order every time. I'll have known that already, of course. Tonight we learned the first bars of the tune.",
    alembist: "Ailment: an ogre with a kitchen, and a kitchen with opinions. Note for the shelf: everything he serves is labelled in advance. We were not. Two small fires, neither of them mine.",
    threadweaver: "That ogre ran us ragged round his greasy kitchen, and my little stitches wandered off to every corner of it. Poor needlework all round. Tomorrow the whole hem stays tight.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "Grubnak is down. Every course, every cleaver, and the line held through all of it. Good footing at last. Held.",
    gloomsworn: "The Cook is down. Every course came for the line, and the line was still standing for the last one. A post well kept is the only rest I know. The watch is relieved.",
    thresher: "Grubnak came in at last, the whole long service of him, and went over gently onto the soft ground of his own kitchen. Every course came due and we stood in the right row for each. A good row, start to finish. I'll miss him a little.",
    bloodreaver: "The big cook fell at last, and I have a scar from every course of his. A feast of a fight! Shame it's over. Best meal I ever bled for.",
    runeguard: "The Chef has fallen. Every omen came in its proper order, and every answer was ready a breath before it was needed. As foreseen, finally.",
    gorgemaw: "My host is finished, and so is his menu: every course eaten, every dish sent back to him in pieces. A long and heavy meal. Still hungry.",
    oathbroker: "The Ogre Chef's account is closed: every course paid in full by the undersigned, and his own debt collected at the last. Countersigned and entered in the ledger.",
    ironpalm: "The cook said everything he had to say. We answered every word. Then he was quiet. I breathed.",
    ranger: "Ogre's down. Took the whole service and every lane I could find. Clean line, start to finish.",
    shadowblade: "The mark served his last course and toppled face first into it. He spent the whole evening glaring at our knight. It never saw me.",
    pyromancer: "We beat Grubnak! I burned brighter than his whole stove, and this time the fire did what I told it. Eyebrows: present, nearly.",
    thermist: "Down. Kitchen cooling. I ran hot all night and somehow landed right in the middle every time. Back to the middle, and stayed there.",
    driftblade: "We went through every course he had and out the other side, and he dropped mid-swing, and I didn't land once all evening.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man has taken his final bow, face down in the pudding. The company was magnificent, my Doll unforgettable. A triumphant closing night. (Curtain.)",
    cleric: "The cook is gone, and every one of us walked out of his kitchen. I lit a candle by the cold stove for those who didn't, before us. Thank the light.",
    grovekeeper: "The old ogre's season is over. My lot weathered every course, roots down, and the kitchen is quiet now, like the morning after a storm. Plant first. It worked.",
    chronomancer: "Grubnak fell on the last bar, as he was always going to. I'd say I knew, but this time I truly didn't have to undo a thing.",
    alembist: "Ailment: one ogre chef. Remedy: patience, labels, and a party that stood where I told it. Shelves nearly empty. Only one explosion, and it was celebratory.",
    threadweaver: "The ogre's down and not one thread snapped through the whole service. My little stitches held together from first course to last. Pulled it tight.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Grease Fire': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He raised his arms and the slick under the whole kitchen went up at once. Nothing to block. We drew in close to the healer, mended before it caught, and stood through it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He raised his arms and the oil went up under all of us. There is nothing to stand behind, so the line gathered at our healer and was mended before the flames rose, not after. I have paid for worse. The watch held.",
      thresher: "When Grubnak raised his arms the oil came up under the whole field at once, like frost on every furrow. No row escapes weather like that, so we drew in round our healer before it lit and were mended ahead of it. Plenty of time, standing together.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole floor flared at once. Barely a scratch for me, but the soft ones felt it, so we huddled by the healer before the grease caught. Good warm burn, that.",
      runeguard: "The omen: his arms rise and the oil sheen widens beneath us all. The answer: no step escapes it; gather by the healer and be whole before it catches. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host doused the whole floor in oil and set light to it, a starter for every guest at once. No plate to send back, so we sat close and swallowed it together. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "The fine print of Grease Fire: it is owed by every co-signatory at once and cannot be dodged. So we gathered by the healer, made good our wounds in advance, and paid in common. Settled.",
      ironpalm: "His arms rose. He was speaking to all of us. We gathered close, whole before he finished, and let the fire pass. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Oil across the whole floor, then fire. No lane out of it. Closed in on the healer before it lit and took it together.",
      shadowblade: "When he lifted his arms the oil ran under everyone, which was frankly unkind. There is no hiding from it, so I slipped in beside the healer, had my scratches mended beforehand, and smiled through the smoke.",
      pyromancer: "When the oil sheen rolled out under all of us, I learned there is no outrunning it, so I kept casting right beside the healer and let the flames come to me. Only my hem smouldered.",
      thermist: "Cold read: arms up, oil everywhere, no edge to step past. Gather. Then the heat hit all of us and I flared, but we were together and whole, and it washed through. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "There was no way through when the oil went down under all of us, which I hated, so I drifted in close to the healer and stood still for once and let it burn through and out.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man raised his arms and the whole stage went up. No wings to hide in; the company gathered centre, mended beforehand, and took it together. (The Doll, merely toasted.)",
      cleric: "He raised his arms and the grease beneath everyone caught alight. I drew them all close and laid the light on them before the flames rose, not after. Nobody fell. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Grease Fire comes on like a dry summer: the whole floor at once. I gathered my lot close and planted my mending a breath before it caught. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Arms up, oil out, fire a beat later; everyone burns, every time. We gathered on the upbeat and I had them mended before it caught. Next time, which was this time, nobody needed undoing.",
      alembist: "Ailment: oil under the whole party, lit a moment after his arms go up. Remedy: everyone gathered near me, wounds closed beforehand. Note: no dose cures it after; brew it first.",
      threadweaver: "The grease caught under every one of my little stitches at once. I pulled the whole hem tight around our healer before it lit, and we shared the burn between us. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Mince': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One cleaver caught the light. That whole side of him came down. I kept him facing steady and the line stepped across to the other half before it fell. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "One cleaver caught the light and that half of the kitchen came down. I kept the Cook looking at me while the line stepped onto the dull half before it fell. Nobody paid.",
      thresher: "One cleaver caught the light, and that half of the kitchen was the ripe half, the half coming due. I walked over to the dull half before it fell and kept working from there. Read the glint, cross the row.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook's cleaver glinted on one side and chopped that whole half of the room. Tempting, but I stepped across to the dull side like a good lad. Saving the scar for later.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a cleaver catching the light on one side of him. The answer: that entire half is forfeit; cross to the other before it falls. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host flashed one cleaver and minced everything on that side of him. Crude knife-work. I took my seat on the other half of the table and watched. Underseasoned.",
      oathbroker: "Mince: the glinting cleaver names the half of the room that pays. By the same clause, the other half owes nothing. The undersigned moved accordingly. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A cleaver glinted. It said: this side. I answered by standing on the other. It fell where I was not.",
      ranger: "Watched which cleaver glinted. That half of the room got chopped. Stood on the other side of its line. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "One cleaver glittered at the mark's side, practically announcing itself. That half of the floor is his; the other half is mine. I strolled across and it chopped empty air. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A cleaver glinted at Grubnak's side, the colour of trouble, and that whole half of the kitchen got minced. I hopped to the dull side and kept casting. Not a sleeve lost.",
      thermist: "Cold read: which cleaver shines. That half dies. Cross. Then he chopped and I was already swinging back into him from the safe side, burning. The shiny side is too far.",
      driftblade: "One cleaver flashed, so I went round him and through to the other half, and the chop came down on nothing behind me, and I was back at his side before the dust settled.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man flashes one cleaver, and that half of the stage is struck. The company exits to the other half on cue. (The Doll, on whichever side does not glint, unscathed.)",
      cleric: "One of his cleavers flashed, and everything on that side of him was chopped. Our young archer lingered on the wrong half once; never again. Read the glint, cross over. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Mince splits the kitchen like a plough splits a field. The glinting cleaver marks the half that gets turned. My lot crossed to the other furrow early. Plant first, move first.",
      chronomancer: "The cleaver glints, then a beat, then that whole half is gone. I'd crossed to the dull side before the glint had finished. I always will have.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one half of the kitchen, chopped. Label: the cleaver that glints. Remedy: stand in the other half before it falls. I wrote it on the shelf in large letters.",
      threadweaver: "A cleaver winked on one side of that ogre and he chopped that whole half clean off the pattern. I gathered my little stitches on the other half before it fell. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Double Chop': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the harder nights he chops both halves. The glinting side first, the other right after. We stood on the dull side, then crossed into the ground he'd just cut. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights he cuts both halves, the glinting one first and the dull one after. We stood on the dull side, then crossed into the ground he had just cut. The only gap in that kitchen is behind the blade.",
      thresher: "On the harder nights he cuts the field twice, the glinting half first, then the dull. I waited on the dull side, and when the first fell I stepped onto that fresh stubble. Ground that's just been cut doesn't come due again.",
      bloodreaver: "Both halves of the room, one after the other! I waited on the dull side, and the instant the glinting half came down I walked straight into it. Clever, my friend. Not clever enough.",
      runeguard: "Not foreseen; now it is. The glinting half falls first and its ghost follows on the other side; wait on the dull side, then cross into the freshly cut half. The order is the answer.",
      gorgemaw: "My host, unsure of his portions, chopped both halves of the table in turn. I sat on the dull side for the first course and moved to the cleared plate for the second. Overworked.",
      oathbroker: "Double Chop carries a second instalment: the glinting half is due first, the other half after. The undersigned waited on the later account, then moved to the settled one. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "He said this side, then that side. I stood where he would speak second, then moved to where he had already spoken. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Two chops. Glinting half first, then the other. Waited on the dull side, crossed in behind the first cut.",
      shadowblade: "The mark chopped one half, then the other, like a man who can't decide. I waited on the dull side and slipped across into the cut half the moment it fell. Indecision is so unbecoming.",
      pyromancer: "He chopped both halves of the kitchen! I stood on the dull side, then dashed into the glinting half the instant it was done. Two chops, no eyebrows lost. Well, one.",
      thermist: "Cold: glint side, then the ghost behind it. Wait on the ghost side. Hot: first chop lands, cross over, burning, as the second slams down behind me. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "He chops one half and then the other, so I waited on the dull side, hating it, then went through into the cut ground the moment it fell, and the second chop landed in my wake.",
      marionettist: "A two-scene number: our leading man chops the glinting half, then the other. The company waits in the second, then crosses into the first. (The Doll, crossing on cue, to applause.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights the cleaver falls twice: the glinting half, then the other. Wait on the dull side, then step into the cut ground after the first. I watched everyone manage it. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Double Chop is a field turned twice. The glinting furrow first, the other after. My lot waited in the second and stepped into the first once it was turned, like rotating crops.",
      chronomancer: "Glinting half, then the echo on the other. I waited out the first beat on the dull side and was already in the cut half for the second. I'll have done it a dozen times by now.",
      alembist: "Ailment: both halves chopped in turn. Remedy: wait in the half that doesn't glint, then cross into the half that just fell. Note: order matters, as with reagents.",
      threadweaver: "The glinting half first, the other right after. My little stitches waited on the dull side, then I pulled them all across into the cut half at once. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Ladle Spin': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He whirled the ladle round himself and anything close got scalded. I stepped back off him with the line and came straight back in once it stopped. Gave ground on purpose.",
      gloomsworn: "He whirled the ladle round himself and everything near him was scalded. I gave the ground up on purpose, the line with me, and came back in the moment he stopped. A watch can step back and still be kept.",
      thresher: "Grubnak whirled his ladle and everything near him got scalded, like a wind laying flat the near rows. I stepped back past the edge of it, leaned on my handle a moment, and walked back in when it stopped. Plenty of time.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook spun his ladle and the soup flew in a ring round him. I backed off for once. Hated it! Then straight back in for the next good hit.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the ladle swung low and soup churning round his feet. The answer: step outside the ring before it turns; return once it has. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host served soup by flinging it round in a circle. I declined, stepped back past the splash, and returned for the next course. Too thin.",
      oathbroker: "Ladle Spin: the clause binds only those who stand close. The undersigned stepped beyond its terms and returned once it lapsed. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The ladle said: step away. I stepped away. When it had finished, I came back. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Ladle out, ring of soup round its feet. Backed off past the edge. Came back in when it stopped.",
      shadowblade: "The mark spun his ladle in a tidy circle, as if his back needed defending. I stepped out of reach, waited politely, then returned to it. It never saw me leave.",
      pyromancer: "Ladle Spin! Scalding soup round his feet, so I stepped well outside the ring and kept casting from there. I'm always outside anyway. Splash-free, mostly.",
      thermist: "Ladle low, soup churning. Step out. Wait. Then I swung right back into his side, hot again, before the steam had cleared.",
      driftblade: "He spun his ladle, so I slipped out past the edge of the soup and round and back in through the steam the instant it stopped, never landed.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man's ladle number: a ring of hot soup at centre stage. The company exits upstage until the spin ends. (The Doll, briefly in the wings.)",
      cleric: "When he swings the ladle, everything near him is scalded. Our brave knight and the close fighters stepped back out of the ring, and I didn't have to mend a single burn. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The ladle spin is a little whirlwind at his feet. Easy weather to read: step out past the churned ground, wait, step back. My lot learned it quickly.",
      chronomancer: "The ladle comes round once: out on the wind-up, back in on the beat after. A short, dull little tune. I'll have heard it a hundred times.",
      alembist: "Ailment: scalding soup in a ring around him. Remedy: stand outside that ring until the ladle stops. Note: soup, unlike brandy, does not explode. Pity.",
      threadweaver: "The ladle came round and my little stitches all stepped back out of the soup at once, nice and even, then came back in together. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Lid Slam': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lifted the lid overhead and everything away from him took the slam. I stayed under his chin and the line closed in behind me. Under him is the safe ground. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The lid went up and everything away from him took the slam. The only safe ground is under his chin, so that is where I stood, and the line closed in behind me. The dark taught me to stand where the fear is.",
      thresher: "When the lid went up, the whole kitchen away from him came due at once, every row but the one under his chin. So I tucked in right against him, close as a gleaner at the binder's heels, and let it come down on empty ground.",
      bloodreaver: "The lid went up and the shock rolled out over the whole room except right at his belly. So in we came, nose to apron with my friend. Cosy.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the lid held aloft and its shadow rippling outward. The answer: the only quiet ground is close beneath him; come in before it falls. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host lifted the lid on his dish and smacked the whole room with it, except those seated right at his elbow. I always sit close to the kitchen. Underwhelming.",
      oathbroker: "Lid Slam: the obligation falls on all who keep their distance; those within arm's reach owe nothing. The undersigned closed in. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "The lid rose. It said: come close. We came close. It struck everything else.",
      ranger: "Lid up. Everywhere hit but right under it. Gave up my range and walked in. Ugly. Worked.",
      shadowblade: "The lid rose over the mark's head and the whole room beyond him was doomed. So I came in close, right behind him, where I'd rather be anyway. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Lid Slam! Everywhere away from him gets flattened, so I had to run in right beside the ogre, who smells awful. Then back out to burn. Hair: gravy-scented.",
      thermist: "Lid overhead. Cold read: the quiet middle is under him. Close in. Then the slam rippled out past us and I was hot and cutting before it reached the walls.",
      driftblade: "The lid rose and the safe place was in close, so I went straight in under it, nearly through his apron strings, and the slam rolled out through the room behind me.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man raises the lid; the company rushes downstage into his very shadow, and the slam takes the empty house. (The Doll, under his elbow, quite safe.)",
      cleric: "When he raised the lid, the safe place was right up against him, which frightened some of us. I walked in with them, and the slam passed over our heads. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The lid slam is like hail: it falls everywhere but under the old tree. My lot crowded in at his feet before it fell. Funny shelter, that ogre.",
      chronomancer: "Lid up, everyone in, then the slam rolls out past us. The middle is quiet every time. I'll have been standing there already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: everything away from him slammed flat. Remedy: stand close underneath. Note: counterintuitive. Label it twice.",
      threadweaver: "The lid went up and I drew every thread in tight to the ogre's feet before it fell. Anyone slack at the edges would have been flattened. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Tenderize': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The big cleaver came for me alone, and it cut the whole sweep in front of him. I waited for the arm to rise, braced, and kept the line behind him and to the sides. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The big cleaver came for me, and it cuts the whole sweep in front of him. I waited for the arm to rise, drew the dark about me, and kept the line behind him and to the sides, never in front. A blow meant for me is a debt I will pay.",
      thresher: "The big cleaver was meant for our shield, and it takes the whole wedge in front of him like a scythe through standing corn. I kept to his side and behind, out of that row, and let our knight take what was due him.",
      bloodreaver: "Tenderize! A good one, right to the bone. I braced, took it full, then drank it straight back. The others kept out of the sweep in front of him. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the great cleaver turning to follow whoever stands before him. The answer: a ward laid a breath before it falls, and everyone else well clear of the sweep in front. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host's signature dish, served straight to me: the whole cleaver. I opened wide just before it landed and swallowed it, while my friends kept away from the front of the table. Tough, but meaty.",
      oathbroker: "Tenderize is payable by whoever stands before the Chef, and any bystander in the sweep is liable too. I accepted delivery, deferred the worst, and kept my co-signatories off the front. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The great cleaver came. I waited until its last instant to raise my hands, and took less. The others stood aside. Then I answered.",
      ranger: "Heavy cleaver for whoever's in front. Cuts the whole wedge before it. Kept out of that, on its flank. The knight braced.",
      shadowblade: "Tenderize is the mark's love letter to whoever stands in front of him, and anyone else in that sweep gets a copy. I stay behind, where love letters never reach. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Grubnak hits whoever stands in front of him with a cleaver the size of a door, and it cuts the whole wedge before him! I stayed out of that wedge. Our knight braced. My eyebrows lived.",
      thermist: "Cold read: cleaver turning to follow the front. Clear the wedge. Then it fell on our shield, hard, and I ran hot with worry, but he held.",
      driftblade: "The big cleaver sweeps the front of him, and our shield took it full while I went round through his side and kept clear of that wedge, never landing in it.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man's big moment, delivered to whoever plays opposite him. The rest of the company keeps well out of the front of the stage. (The Doll, upstage, admiring.)",
      cleric: "The heavy cleaver falls on our brave knight, as it always does, and on anyone foolish enough to stand in front beside him. I laid the light on our knight before it fell. Dawn keep him.",
      grovekeeper: "Tenderize comes on like a sudden squall off the front of him. I planted my mending on our shield before the cleaver dropped, and my lot kept out of that wedge. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Cleaver up, a held beat, then down on whoever's in front, and on anyone else in the sweep. I'll have mended the knight already. Everyone else stays out of the front.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one tremendous cleaver on whoever stands before him, and on anyone else in the wedge. Remedy: the dose given to our shield before it falls. Note: everyone else, out of the front.",
      threadweaver: "The cleaver fell on our knight, and I'd already tied his thread to mine, so some of the weight ran down the seam to me. Everyone else kept clear of the front. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Meat Toss': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Grubnak hung a slab over each of the others. Each needed its own patch of floor, and each left grease behind. They dropped it out at the edges, apart, away from the line. Footing kept. The stain fades in time, shrinking and dimming first, and it is gone altogether if fire reaches it.",
      gloomsworn: "Slabs hung over each of the others, each wanting its own patch of floor and each leaving grease where it landed. They went to the edges, apart, away from the line. The stain thins in time, and fire takes it at once. I remember where each fell.",
      thresher: "A slab hung over my head, ripening. I carried it off to the wall, away from the others, and let it drop there, a greasy patch on ground nobody works. It fades in its season, and fire takes it at once, so I mind where it lies.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook hung steaks over the others, never me, insulting. They each took their own corner and left the grease out by the walls, not under my feet. Clean floor to bleed on. The stains fade after a while, shrinking and dimming first, or burn off if fire reaches them.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a steak on a chain over each of us save the one before him. The answer: walk apart to the edge and let the grease fall there; it lingers, and later it burns. As foreseen. The stain does not last for ever: it dims and shrinks, then is gone.",
      gorgemaw: "My host dangled steaks over my friends but not me, which I took personally. They carried their plates apart, to the walls, so the grease wouldn't pool under the table. Greasy. The stains fade in the end, shrinking and dimming first.",
      oathbroker: "Meat Toss: each co-signatory but the shield receives a separate account, payable apart, with a lien of grease left behind. We settled out by the walls. The fine print: the stains wait for fire. The fine print: the stains fade in the end, shrinking and dimming first, unless fire gets there sooner.",
      ironpalm: "A steak hung over each of the others. Each walked alone to the edge. The grease stayed there, where nobody needed to stand. The stain faded in the end.",
      ranger: "Steak over my head. Walked to the wall, clear of the others. Puddle stayed there, out of the lanes. Remember where. Stain fades in time, shrinks and dims first.",
      shadowblade: "The mark dangled a steak over me, terribly forward of him. I carried it off to a quiet corner, away from everyone, and left my little grease puddle where nobody walks. Tidy. The stains fade in the end, which is a shame, they were rather nice.",
      pyromancer: "A steak hung over my head like a prize! I carried it to the wall, away from the others, and dropped my grease there, because grease and I are a bad combination. Mostly unsinged. The stains fade in the end, shrinking and dimming first, or burn away if fire reaches them.",
      thermist: "Cold read: steak over me. Walk to the edge, apart from the others. Drop it. The grease stays, and when fire comes it burns, so out of the way is the only way. The stain fades in time, dims and shrinks first.",
      driftblade: "A steak hung over me, so I carried it round through the gaps between the others to the far wall and dropped it there, out of every path I'd want to run through later. The stains fade in time, so the floor clears itself eventually.",
      marionettist: "A steak lowered over each player but the lead. Each exits to the wings, apart from the others, and drops the grease offstage. (The Doll, carefully not underneath anything.) The stains fade in time, dimming and shrinking first.",
      cleric: "The steaks hang over everyone but our knight. Each of us went to our own place by the wall, so no two puddles touched, and the grease stayed where nobody had to walk. Thank the light. The stains fade in time, dimming and shrinking first.",
      grovekeeper: "Meat Toss sows grease. Each of my lot carried theirs to the edge of the field and planted it there, apart, where it wouldn't spoil the paths. The stains linger, and later they burn. The stains fade in time, shrinking and dimming first.",
      chronomancer: "Steaks over all but the knight; each walks to their own spot by the wall and drops it. The grease stays, and burns later. I'll have chosen my spot last time; I chose it again. The stains fade in time, shrinking and dimming first.",
      alembist: "Ailment: steaks over all but our shield, each leaving grease, then a stain that waits for fire. Remedy: each to their own spot by the wall. Note: grease is a reagent. Label where it went. Note: the stains fade in time, shrinking and dimming first.",
      threadweaver: "Steaks over my little stitches. I loosened every thread so each could walk to the wall alone and leave their grease out of the way. Then back together. Mind the slack. The stains fade in time, shrinking and dimming first.",
    } },
    'Family Meal': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A silver cloche came down over one of ours. One plate for the whole table. The line gathered under it and shared the blow; I joined them when I could. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A silver cloche came down over one of our own, and one plate does not feed a table. We went under it together and split the blow between us, and I stood in the ring too. Shared, it is a small debt for each.",
      thresher: "A silver cloche came down over one of our lot, and one portion for the whole table is lighter split. We all walked in under it like hands in from the rain and took it together. Shared weather is easy weather.",
      bloodreaver: "Family Meal! One big plate, split between us all. We crowded in under the cloche, shoulder to shoulder, and I made sure I was there for my share. Hurts less shared, though. Shame.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a silver cloche lowered over one of us. The answer: all gather beneath it and divide the blow; the fewer present, the heavier each share. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Family Meal, a platter for the whole table! My friends gathered under the cloche and I joined them, and we shared it out. I'd have eaten it all alone, frankly. Rich, but shared.",
      oathbroker: "Family Meal: a joint liability. All the undersigned gathered under the cloche and divided the debt between us; any who absent themselves raise the others' share. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The cloche fell over one of us. It asked for all of us. We gathered and answered together.",
      ranger: "Cloche over one of us. Everyone into it. Shared the hit. Hate crowding. Necessary.",
      shadowblade: "A cloche descended over a friend, and the whole company had to crowd in beneath it to share the portion. Not my usual company, but I squeezed in. One must dine with someone.",
      pyromancer: "A silver cloche dropped over the young archer and we all had to crowd in to share the blow. Very cosy. Very warm. This time I singed somebody else's sleeve.",
      thermist: "Cold: cloche over one of us. Gather in. Then it landed and split between all of us and I barely warmed. Back to the middle, together.",
      driftblade: "The cloche came down over one of us and we all had to stand still under it together, which I despise, so I circled until the last breath, slipped in through the gap and took my share.",
      marionettist: "A silver cloche over one member of the company: an ensemble number. Everyone onstage beneath it, sharing the line. (The Doll, front and centre, naturally.)",
      cleric: "Family Meal is served to one, and the rest of us must come to the table, or that one takes it all. I gathered everyone in under the cloche and we shared it. Grace before meals.",
      grovekeeper: "Family Meal is a harvest for the whole table. My lot gathered close under the cloche and split it between us, roots together. Nobody went hungry, nobody fell.",
      chronomancer: "The cloche goes down over one; on the beat everyone's under it with them, and the blow is split. I'll have been there first. I usually am.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one heavy portion, served to one. Remedy: dilute it. Everyone under the cloche, so it divides between us. Note: dilution works on most things.",
      threadweaver: "A cloche came down over one of my stitches, and I drew every thread tight to bring the whole hem in under it. Shared between us all, it barely stung. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Larder Toss': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He hurled casks at the two of ours standing farthest off. They stepped out from under before they landed and left the casks on open floor. Solid cover. Kept them for the next blast.",
      gloomsworn: "He hurled casks at the two of ours standing farthest off. They stepped out from under at the last and left the casks on open floor, where they stay as cover. Not near the Cook, or he catches them.",
      thresher: "He flung casks at the two of us farthest out. Whoever's it was led it onto open ground, stepped off at the last, and left it standing like a stook in the stubble, good shelter later. Not near Grubnak, mind, or he catches it.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook threw barrels at whoever stood furthest off. They dodged; pity, a barrel to the face is honest work. The casks stayed where they fell, and those were worth keeping.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the shelf torn loose and a circle trailing the two farthest of us. The answer: carry it somewhere useful, step aside before it lands, and keep the cask; it is shelter for what follows. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host flung his own larder at the two guests farthest from the table. They let the casks land beside them, not on them, and kept them as furniture. Poor service, good barrels.",
      oathbroker: "Larder Toss: delivery to the two co-signatories farthest from the Chef. They signed for it at a sensible address, stood aside on arrival, and kept the goods. The fine print: the cask is collateral against the roar.",
      ironpalm: "He threw a cask to the farthest of us. They stepped aside. It stayed. Something to stand behind, later.",
      ranger: "Cask came at me for standing furthest out. Led it to open floor, stepped off at the last. It stays. Good cover. Not too near the ogre, or it catches it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark threw a cask at me for keeping my distance, which is hardly fair. I placed it neatly on open ground, stepped aside, and kept it. A lady needs something to hide behind.",
      pyromancer: "He threw a cask at me! Me! I led it somewhere sensible, out of the fire, hopped clear, and then had a lovely barrel to hide behind for the roar. Splinter-free.",
      thermist: "Cold: I'm farthest, so a cask is mine. Lead it to clear floor, out of the fire, not too near him. Step off. Then it sat there, solid, and I knew exactly what was coming.",
      driftblade: "A cask came for me because I'd drifted farthest out, so I swung it round to open floor and slipped out from under at the last breath, and it stayed there, which mattered a moment later.",
      marionettist: "Props flung from the larder at the two players farthest upstage. They step aside on cue and leave the scenery standing; it matters in the next scene. (The Doll, admiring the set dressing.)",
      cleric: "The two who stand farthest from him have a cask thrown at them. They led it to open floor, stepped out before it fell, and left it standing. Those casks saved us all a little later.",
      grovekeeper: "Larder Toss plants casks in the field. Whoever's farthest out leads them to good ground, steps aside before they fall, and lets them stand. Cover is planted like a hedge.",
      chronomancer: "Casks at the two farthest, a beat after the shelf comes loose. Step aside before it lands and leave it where it's useful. The roar comes after. It always comes after.",
      alembist: "Ailment: casks thrown at the two of us farthest off. Remedy: step out from under, but land them somewhere useful. Note for the shelf: casks are not waste. They are shelter. Keep them.",
      threadweaver: "The two stitches farthest out got a cask each. I let their threads out so they could lead the casks to clear floor and step aside. Then we had somewhere to hide. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Gut Blast': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He roared across the whole kitchen. No shield blocks that. I put a cask between myself and Grubnak like the rest of the line and kept still behind it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He roared through the whole kitchen. Nothing stops it but something solid, so a cask went between me and him, and the line crouched behind their own. Even I hide from some things.",
      thresher: "His belly swelled, and I've watched enough storms come in to know that one reaches the whole field. Only something solid stops it, so I put a cask between me and him, or the pot later on, and sat out the gale behind it.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook bellowed so hard it shook the walls. Even I hid behind a barrel for that one; it's a scar with nothing in it. Cask between me and him, then back to work.",
      runeguard: "The omen: his belly swelling, breath drawn deep. The answer: a cask, or later the pot, set squarely between oneself and him; the blast leaves a shadow behind each. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host belched at the whole dining room. Revolting manners. I ducked behind a cask with my friends, him on the far side, and let the stench pass. Sent it back.",
      oathbroker: "Gut Blast is owed by everyone not sheltered. The clause exempts any party behind a cask, or the pot, with the Chef on the far side. The undersigned found shelter. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He breathed in. I stepped behind a cask. He shouted. It went past me. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Roar covers the whole kitchen. Only cover is a cask, or the pot later. Put it between me and the ogre. No line, for once.",
      shadowblade: "When the mark drew breath to roar, I tucked myself behind a cask with it squarely between us. A roar can't touch what it can't see. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Gut Blast is the loudest thing I have ever heard, and I once blew up a library! Hide behind a cask, or the pot later, with Grubnak on the far side. Hair blown back. Not burnt.",
      thermist: "Cold: find a cask. Put it between me and him. Low. Then the roar came and its heat went round me on both sides and never touched me. Too close.",
      driftblade: "His roar fills the whole room, so the only answer is a cask, and I swung in behind one with him on the far side and stayed perfectly still while it howled round me.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man's great aria: a roar that fills the whole house. The company takes cover behind the scenery, the casks, with him on the far side. (The Doll, behind a barrel, mouthing along.)",
      cleric: "When he draws in that great breath, everyone must hide behind a cask with him on the far side. Anyone caught in the open is hurt terribly. I kept our people behind the barrels. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Gut Blast blows like a gale off the moor. My lot sheltered in the lee of the casks, or the pot when there was one, with him upwind. Every windbreak counts.",
      chronomancer: "Belly swells, a long held beat, then the roar. Cask between you and him before the beat ends. I'll have learned that the hard way once. Once was enough.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one roar, the whole kitchen. Remedy: a cask, or the pot after the fire, between you and him. Note: each blast cracks the casks, and on the harder nights a cask shelters only two.",
      threadweaver: "When he drew his breath, I pulled every one of my stitches behind a cask, him on the far side. Anyone left hanging out in the open would have heard from me. A neat seam.",
    } },
    "Butcher's Charge": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lowered his head at me and charged till he hit something. I put bare brick behind me, stepped aside at the last, and he hit the wall and stood dazed. The line kept off his path. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He put his head down at me and ran. I set bare brick at my back and waited, then slid aside at the last breath. He met the wall and stood dazed. The others kept off his road. I have been a door for worse.",
      thresher: "He put his head down at our knight and ran like a bull through a fence. I kept off that line, and when our knight led him into the brick and he stood there dazed, that was my moment for the cut. Ripe as anything.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook lowered his head and came for me! I led him at bare brick and stepped aside, and he hit it like a drunk. Stuck and dazed, so I carved into him. Not the casks, not the stove.",
      runeguard: "The omen: his head lowered toward me and a path drawn to the far wall. The answer: aim it at brick, never the stove or our casks, and step aside once it sets. Dazed, he bleeds more freely. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host charged headfirst at me like a bull at a gate. I stood with bare brick at my back and stepped aside, and he ate the wall instead. Stuck and dazed. A fitting course for him.",
      oathbroker: "Butcher's Charge: the Chef runs on until met by something solid. I nominated bare brick, neither the stove nor our casks, and stepped aside once the terms were fixed. Dazed, he defaulted. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He lowered his head at me. I gave him a wall to speak to, then stepped aside. He listened to the wall for a while.",
      ranger: "It lowers its head at the knight and runs until it hits something. Keep off the line. Brick stops it dazed. Good time to shoot.",
      shadowblade: "The mark lowered its head at our knight, who led it into a brick wall. Delightful. The rest of us kept off the line, and while it sat there dazed I took my time with its back.",
      pyromancer: "He charged our knight, who aimed him at bare brick! Not the stove, not our casks. Everyone kept off his line, and then he was stuck there seeing stars, and I poured everything into him.",
      thermist: "Cold: his line to the knight. Off it. Brick behind the knight. Then he went, hot and fast, and hit the wall, and I was all over him while he swayed.",
      driftblade: "He lowers his head and runs until something stops him, so we put bare brick at the end and I cut through off his line, and he hit the wall dazed and I circled him the whole while.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man charges the lead player, who steps aside and lets him meet the back wall of the set. Not the stove, not the scenery. (The Doll, applauding the stunt work.)",
      cleric: "He lowers his head at our knight and runs until he hits something. Our knight learned to lead him into bare brick, where he stands dazed, and everyone else kept clear of his path. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The charge is a bull through a fence. Our shield turned him toward bare brick, not the stove or the casks, and my lot stepped off his path. He stood dazed after, like a struck ox.",
      chronomancer: "Head down, the path follows the knight, and a beat before he goes it stops following. Aim at brick, step aside on that beat. He'll have been dazed. He was. Lovely long pause.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one ogre in a straight line, until he hits something. Remedy: aim him at bare brick, off our line, never the stove or our casks. Note: the stove erupts. I wrote that one twice.",
      threadweaver: "He charges our knight until he hits something. I pulled my little stitches off his line, and our knight brought him round to a bare wall, not the casks, not the stove. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Chop Chop': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "One cleaver glinted, and the ladle or lid on his back said what came after. We crossed to the dull half, then went out for the ladle or in for the lid. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "One cleaver caught the light, and the ladle or lid on his back told the rest. We crossed to the half that stayed dull, then went out for the ladle or in for the lid. Read both before the first step.",
      thresher: "A glint on one cleaver and a tool on his back, both coming due, one after the other, like haying and then rain. I crossed to the dull half, then went out for the ladle or in for the lid. Two crops ripening at once; I read both first.",
      bloodreaver: "Chop, then whatever's on his back! Glinting half first, so I took the other side. Then the lid meant in, the ladle meant out. Two lessons in one breath, my friend.",
      runeguard: "The omen comes twice: the glinting cleaver names the half, the tool on his back names what follows. The answer: the dull half first, then in for the lid or out for the ladle. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A two-course dish: first the minced half, then whatever hung on my host's back, ladle or lid. I sat on the dull side, then took the right seat for the second. Hurried, but edible.",
      oathbroker: "Chop Chop is a compound clause: the glinting half is due first, and the tool on his back states the terms of the second, ladle without, lid within. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke twice. The glint: this half. The tool: near, or far. I answered both.",
      ranger: "Glinting side gets chopped. Then the tool on its back: ladle, out; lid, in. Read both before moving.",
      shadowblade: "The mark announces both courses at once: the glinting cleaver for the half, the tool on his back for what follows. I took the dull half, then followed the tool. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Two dishes at once! The glinting cleaver chops a half, then the tool on his back follows: ladle, get out; lid, get in. I managed both and only dropped one spell. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: glint, half; back, tool. Dull side. Then hot: the ladle meant out, so out, and then straight back in, burning. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The glint tells you which half and the tool on his back tells you what next, so I went through to the dull half and out of the ladle's ring and back in through the steam.",
      marionettist: "A scene with a quick change: the glinting cleaver strikes one half of the stage, then the tool on his back sets the next cue, ladle out, lid in. (The Doll, hitting both marks.)",
      cleric: "Chop Chop asks two things together. The glinting side is chopped, then the tool on his back follows: the ladle wants us out, the lid wants us in. I watched each friend get it right.",
      grovekeeper: "Chop Chop is a frost and then a thaw. The glinting half falls first, then the tool on his back tells the weather: ladle, keep out; lid, come in. My lot read both before moving.",
      chronomancer: "Chop, then the tool a beat behind. The glint picks the half, his back picks in or out. A two-bar phrase. I've heard it so often now that I hum it.",
      alembist: "Ailment: two at once. Label one: the glinting cleaver, that half chopped. Label two: the tool on his back, ladle out, lid in. Remedy: read both labels before pouring.",
      threadweaver: "The glinting half first, then the tool on his back: out for the ladle, in for the lid. I moved my little stitches as one piece, twice. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Order Up!': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Plates round the kitchen, one for each of us, served one after another. Each took their own plate and stood on it until it was served. None left on the counter. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Plates round the kitchen, one for each of us, served in turn. Each of us kept a plate and stood on it until it was taken; none went begging. I wait well. The dead have practice.",
      thresher: "Plates set round the kitchen, one for each of us, coming in one by one like rows at the end of a long day. The fullest rim is the next due. I stood on mine till it was served and left none out in the field.",
      bloodreaver: "A plate for each of us, served in turn round the room. I took mine and stood on it when its rim was full. A plate left alone hurts everyone, and that's not a scar worth having.",
      runeguard: "The omen: cloches round the kitchen, their rims filling at different rates. The answer: each claims one plate; the fullest rim is served next, round from the top left. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host laid out plates for each guest. I took mine, of course, as did my friends, each standing on our own until served. A plate left on the counter is an insult to everyone.",
      oathbroker: "Order Up!: one plate per co-signatory, served in sequence round the room, the fullest rim next. The fine print: an unclaimed plate is charged to all of us. So we each signed for one.",
      ironpalm: "Plates around the room, one for each. Each of us stood on our own until it was served. None went unanswered.",
      ranger: "Plates round the room. One each. Fullest rim lands next, round from the top left. Stood on mine.",
      shadowblade: "Plates laid out round the kitchen, one per guest. I chose mine, stood on it until it was served, and nobody's plate went uncollected. One does have standards.",
      pyromancer: "Plates! A ring of them, one for each of us, served round from the top left; the fullest rim goes next. I stood on mine and cast. A plate left alone hurts everyone.",
      thermist: "Cold: a plate each. Know which before it starts. Fullest rim lands first. Then hot, ran to mine, stood still till it went, ran back. No plate left cold.",
      driftblade: "Plates all round the kitchen, one each, served one after another, and I had to stand still on mine until it was served, then through and away again to the next gap.",
      marionettist: "A plate for each player, laid round the stage and served in sequence, the fullest rim next. Every player stands on their mark until served. (The Doll, on its plate, beaming.)",
      cleric: "Plates on the counter, one for each of us, served round the room. Each of us took one and stood on it in turn. An empty plate hurts all of us. Thank the light, none were left.",
      grovekeeper: "The order rail is a row of seedlings coming up one at a time. Each of my lot tended their own and stood on it till it bloomed. Fullest rim first. Plant first, again.",
      chronomancer: "Plates in a ring, each rim filling toward its own beat, round from the top left. Each of us on our own plate when it's served. I'll have known which was mine last time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one plate per guest, served in order round from the top left. Remedy: assign the plates beforehand; the fullest rim is served first. Note: an unserved plate spoils for everyone.",
      threadweaver: "A plate for each stitch, served round the room in turn. I let the threads out so everyone could reach their own and stand on it. Nobody's plate left bare. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Rolling Meatballs': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He lobbed meatballs against the walls. Each wobbled with its lane drawn across the room, then rolled straight. Kept my footing out of every lane until they'd passed. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Meatballs wobbled at the walls and rolled straight, every lane drawn across the floor. I stood between the lanes and let them pass. The floor tells you where death will roll. It is the one fair thing in that kitchen.",
      thresher: "Meatballs sat wobbling at the walls, each with its furrow drawn straight across the floor ahead of it. I stood on the soft ground between the furrows and watched them roll past like stones down a hillside. Never in a hurry.",
      bloodreaver: "Giant meatballs, rolling across the room! I'd love to be hit by one, but it knocks you sideways and that's undignified. Out of their lanes until they pass.",
      runeguard: "The omen: each meatball lands at the wall and wobbles, its lane drawn across the kitchen before it rolls. The answer: stand clear of every lane until all have passed. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host rolled his meatballs across the dining room instead of serving them. I'd have eaten them had they held still. Stood between the lanes and watched them go. Overcooked.",
      oathbroker: "Rolling Meatballs: each is a debt in transit along a fixed route, drawn before it moves. The undersigned stood outside every route and paid nothing. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Round things rolled toward us. Each showed its path first. I stood where none would go. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Meatballs wobble at the wall, then roll straight across. Lanes drawn. Stood between them.",
      shadowblade: "The mark started bowling meatballs at us. Each sits at the wall and wobbles before it rolls, showing its path. I stood between the paths and watched them go by. Very rude.",
      pyromancer: "Meatballs, enormous ones, rolling straight across the kitchen! Each wobbles first and shows its lane, so I stood clear and kept casting. Not flattened. Hair: gravy, again.",
      thermist: "Cold: meatballs at the walls, wobbling, lanes drawn. Stand between. Then they rolled, hot and fast, and I didn't move a hair until the last one passed.",
      driftblade: "The meatballs wobble at the walls and then roll straight through the room, so I wove between the lanes and through behind each one as it passed, and never got flattened once.",
      marionettist: "Enormous meatballs, rolled across the stage, each along a lane marked in advance. The company stands between the lanes. (The Doll, narrowly missed. Gasps from the house.)",
      cleric: "The great meatballs wobble against the walls before they roll across the room. Stand out of their paths; one knocked our archer clean off his plate. I mended him, and gently scolded.",
      grovekeeper: "Rolling Meatballs come down their lanes like a spring flood down the furrows. Read where each one wobbles and stand between the channels. My lot stayed dry.",
      chronomancer: "They land, they wobble, a beat, then they roll, one after another. Stand between the lanes until the last has passed. I'll have counted them already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: meatballs, large, rolling. Remedy: stand outside every lane; each wobbles first and shows its path. Note: when they come with the plates, the plates sit between the lanes.",
      threadweaver: "The meatballs roll straight across the room once they've wobbled at the wall. I let my little stitches out between their lanes and gathered them back once they'd gone. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Flare-Up': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He heaved the stockpot onto the stove and the whole top of the kitchen caught. It never went out. We were clear of that ground before it caught, braced for the heat. Less floor after. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He heaved the stockpot onto the stove and the top of the kitchen caught, for good. We were off that ground before it lit and braced for the heat. A smaller room, but the line was whole.",
      thresher: "He heaved the stockpot onto the stove and the top of the kitchen went up like a field burnt off after the cut, and it stays burning. I'd walked down out of that end before it caught. A smaller field after, but plenty to work.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook set his own stove alight, the whole top of the room! The heat reached everyone, a good warm scar. Then I stayed out of the fire for the rest of the night.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the stockpot lifted toward the stove. The answer: abandon the top of the kitchen before it catches, for it burns from then on, and have everyone whole for the heat. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host scorched his own kitchen and left us all to stand in the smoke. Out of the top of the room before it caught, then. The heat seasoned everyone. Badly burnt.",
      oathbroker: "Flare-Up: the top of the kitchen is foreclosed for the remainder of the service, and the heat is owed by all. The undersigned vacated before it caught. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He set the stove alight. The top of the room burned. We had left before it spoke. The heat reached us anyway. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Pot onto the stove. Top of the room burns, for good. Out of it before it catches. Smaller room after.",
      shadowblade: "Halfway through, the mark heaved his pot onto the stove and set the top of the kitchen ablaze. I had already left that end of the room. Fire is so terribly common.",
      pyromancer: "He set his own kitchen on fire! Very good fire, too, the colour of real trouble. Out of the top of the room before it catches; it never goes out. I was jealous. Cloak singed.",
      thermist: "Fire all along the top of the room, roaring, and I ran so hot I nearly forgot to move. Cold now: leave the top before it catches. It never goes out. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "He hoisted the pot onto the stove and the whole top of the room went up, so I went down and away through the kitchen before it caught, and after that the room was small and I hated it.",
      marionettist: "The interval: our leading man sets the upper stage alight for the rest of the run. The company moves downstage before it catches, and the heat reaches the stalls. (The Doll, smoking slightly.)",
      cleric: "When he lifts the pot onto the stove, everyone must leave the top of the kitchen; it burns from then on. The heat reaches all of us anyway, so I had everyone mended first. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Flare-Up is the summer wildfire. The top of the kitchen burns from then on, and its heat reaches us all. I moved my lot down the slope before it caught and planted before the heat.",
      chronomancer: "Pot on the stove, a beat, then fire across the top for the rest of the fight, and a wave of heat. We'd have been gone from the top by then. We were.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the top of the kitchen, ablaze for good, and a heat that reaches everyone. Remedy: out of the top before it catches, everyone mended first. Note: smaller workshop now.",
      threadweaver: "He set the whole top of the kitchen alight and it never went out. I drew my stitches down out of it before it caught, and we took the heat close together. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Flambé': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Brandy across the floor in stripes, catching one after another. I held in a stripe that went late, then stepped onto the burnt ground behind the fire. Kept off the old grease. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Brandy was poured across the floor in stripes and lit one after another. I waited in a stripe that would go late, then stepped onto ground the fire had already eaten. Old grease I left alone.",
      thresher: "Brandy laid in stripes across the floor, catching one row after another like a stubble fire. I waited in a late row, then stepped back onto ground already burnt, where nothing's left to catch. And I kept off the old grease.",
      bloodreaver: "Stripes of brandy, catching one after another! I waited, then stepped in behind the flames onto ground already burnt. Grease stains in a stripe go up too. Kept my boots off them.",
      runeguard: "The omen: stripes of brandy, blue flame running along each before it catches. The answer: step into the stripe that has just burnt, behind the fire, and keep clear of old grease. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host flambéed the whole floor, stripe by stripe. Pretentious. I stepped into each stripe once it had burnt and kept off the old grease. Too much brandy.",
      oathbroker: "Flambé: the stripes come due in sequence, and a stripe already paid owes nothing further. The undersigned moved onto settled ground, clear of the grease liens. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "Brandy on the floor. The stripes caught, one by one. I stood where the fire had already spoken.",
      ranger: "Stripes of brandy. They catch in turn. Step into the one already burnt. Keep off grease.",
      shadowblade: "The mark splashed brandy over his floor in stripes and set them alight in turn. I waited for one to burn, then stepped into it, behind the fire. The grease stains catch too; I avoided them, naturally.",
      pyromancer: "Flambé! Stripes of brandy going up one after another! Absolutely beautiful. Step into the stripe that's just burnt and keep off any old grease in it. I wanted to stay and admire. Singed, worth it.",
      thermist: "Brandy, stripes, flame all down the room, and I was burning just watching. Cold: wait. Step into the one that just burnt. Off the grease stains. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The stripes catch one after another, so I ran through behind the fire, into each burnt stripe as it went and round the grease stains, never landing anywhere that hadn't burnt yet.",
      marionettist: "A pyrotechnic interlude: brandy across the stage in stripes, lit in sequence. The company steps into the ones already burnt and keeps off the grease. (The Doll, flame-resistant. Mostly.)",
      cleric: "He splashes brandy across the floor in stripes, and they catch one after another. Wait, then step onto ground that has already burnt; any grease in a stripe goes up with it. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Flambé burns like stubble fires down a field, row by row. Wait, then walk onto the burnt row behind the flames. The old grease we sowed earlier burns too, so keep off it.",
      chronomancer: "The stripes catch in order, a beat apart. I step into each the moment after it's burnt, a tune I'll have been humming already. Old grease catches when its stripe does.",
      alembist: "Ailment: brandy in stripes, catching in turn, and every grease stain in them lights. Remedy: step into a stripe that has already burnt. Note: this is exactly how flambé is meant to work.",
      threadweaver: "Stripes of brandy caught one by one, so I pulled my little stitches into the burnt stripes behind the flames, well away from the old grease. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Stockpot': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the fire he dragged the stockpot down to the middle. It is solid. It stops a charge and shelters from his roar. Marked it as ground to work from. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Once the fire was done he dragged the pot into the middle of the room. It does not move. It stops his charge and keeps his roar off us, so I fought near it. A thing that will not move is a comfort to me.",
      thresher: "After the fire he dragged the stockpot down to the middle and left it, solid as a barn. It turns his charge and keeps his roar off us, so I learned to work near it. Mind its boiling, though; that comes in its season.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook hauled his pot into the middle of the room. Solid as a wall. Good shelter when he roars, and something to ram his face into later.",
      runeguard: "The omen: he leaps to the centre with the stockpot. The answer: it remains there, solid; shelter from his roar and a place to aim his charge. Its rings follow. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host dragged his stockpot to the centre of the table. Finally, a proper centrepiece: solid enough to shelter behind and something to drive him into. The broth smelled of nothing. Bland.",
      oathbroker: "The stockpot has been placed in the middle of the room for the remainder of the service: solid, shelter from the roar, a wall for the charge. The fine print: it boils over. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He set the pot in the middle. It does not move. Something to stand behind. Something that boils.",
      ranger: "Pot in the middle. Solid. Cover from the roar. Can aim the charge at it. Watch its rings.",
      shadowblade: "The mark dragged his pot to the centre and left it there. Solid, wide, opaque. A lovely thing to stand behind. Its rings boil next, though.",
      pyromancer: "He dragged a giant copper pot into the middle of the floor, and it's solid! Something to hide behind when he roars. And it bubbles. I like it.",
      thermist: "Cold: pot in the middle. Solid. Cover. Then the copper went hot and the rings began to boil off it. Watch them.",
      driftblade: "He dragged the pot to the middle and it doesn't move, which I can respect in a pot, so I went round it and behind it and later through its rings.",
      marionettist: "A new set piece, centre stage: the stockpot. Solid scenery, cover for the roar, a target for the charge. (The Doll, leaning on it, very casual.)",
      cleric: "After the fire he hauled the great pot to the middle of the kitchen and left it there. It shelters us from his roar, and our knight can lead his charge into it. Mind the rings it gives off.",
      grovekeeper: "The stockpot settled in the middle like a boulder after a landslide. Solid ground to shelter behind and a place to steer his charge. Rings boil off it after.",
      chronomancer: "Pot to the middle, a leap and a thud. It stays. Cover from the roar, a wall for the charge, and the rings come next, out then in. I'll have remembered.",
      alembist: "Note for the shelf: the stockpot, now in the middle. Uses: shelter from the roar, a wall for the charge. Hazard: it boils over in rings. Label both.",
      threadweaver: "He planted that pot in the middle of the room for good. Something to tie the hem behind when he roars, and our knight can lead his charge into it. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Simmer': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The pot boiled over in rings, out from it, then back in. I held one ring off and stepped in behind each as it boiled. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The pot boiled over in rings, outward and then back in. I stayed outside one ring and stepped in behind it as it passed. Arrive just after the blow, like the dead.",
      thresher: "The pot boiled over in rings, out from it and then back in, like ripples after a stone in the pond. I waited just outside each ring and stepped in behind it once it passed. The ground behind a wave is soft and quiet.",
      bloodreaver: "Rings of boiling water off the pot, out and then back in. I waited, then followed each ring just after it boiled. Barely touched me. Shame.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the coils going dull, red, then white, ring by ring. The answer: step into the ring that has just boiled, outward on the first wave, inward on the second. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host let his pot boil over, ring after ring, out and then in. I followed each ring once it had boiled. The broth was thin. Sent it back.",
      oathbroker: "Simmer: the rings come due in order, outward, then inward. A ring already paid owes nothing, so the undersigned stepped into each behind it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Rings went out from the pot, then came back. I stepped in behind each one. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Rings out from the pot, then rings back in. Step in behind each one as it boils.",
      shadowblade: "The pot boiled over in rings, out and then back in. I simply stepped into each ring after it had boiled, one behind the wave. Patience is a shadow's best friend.",
      pyromancer: "The rings round the pot go dull, then red, then white! I followed each ring after it boiled, outward then back in. Like a dance. A hot dance. Toes: unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: rings, dull, red, white. Wait for white. Step into it. Hot: the next one boils behind me and I'm already on to the next. Out, then back in.",
      driftblade: "The rings boiled outward from the pot, so I went out behind them, through each one as it finished, and then they came back in and I went back through them the other way.",
      marionettist: "Ripples of boiling water from the pot, out then in: a pas de deux with the stove. The company steps into each ring after it boils. (The Doll, dancing.)",
      cleric: "The pot's rings boil one after another, out and then back in. I led our people into each ring just after it boiled, and nobody was scalded. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Simmer comes like ripples in a pond after a stone. Step into each ring just after it passes, out first, then in. My lot rode the ripples.",
      chronomancer: "Rings out, a beat apart, then back in. Step in behind each one as it boils. The second wave is the first, backwards. I'll have noticed that.",
      alembist: "Ailment: rings of boiling water from the pot, outward, then inward. Remedy: step into each ring just after it boils. Note: a pot simmering is how most of my mistakes begin.",
      threadweaver: "The rings boil out from the pot and back in. I kept my little stitches walking just behind each ring as it boiled. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Whisk': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cross of beams through him that turned, a notch at a time. I kept in the gap between beams and walked round with it. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Beams crossed through him and turned a notch at a time. I found the gap between them and walked round with it. A slow danger, and I am patient.",
      thresher: "A cross of beams through him, turning a notch at a time, slow as a millwheel. I stayed in the gap between two arms and walked round with it, keeping my row. No call to hurry; it never does.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook whisked a cross of light through the room, turning step by step. I walked round with the gap, scar-free. Embarrassing.",
      runeguard: "The omen: wire loops forming a cross through him. The answer: stand in the gap between its arms and walk with its turn, keeping pace. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host whisked the air in a cross through the room, turning as he went. I walked round with the gap. All air, no substance. Underseasoned.",
      oathbroker: "Whisk: a cross of beams that turns by stages. The undersigned kept to the gap and moved with each turn. The fine print: walk with it, never against it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The cross turned. I turned with it. It did not touch me.",
      ranger: "Cross through it, turning. Stay in the gap. Walk with the turn.",
      shadowblade: "The mark whisked a cross of beams through the room, turning slowly. I stayed in the gap and walked round with it, the way one keeps to the edge of a mark's gaze.",
      pyromancer: "The Whisk! A cross of beams through him, turning step by step. I walked with the gap and kept casting, dragging my feet only slightly. Hem: intact.",
      thermist: "Cold: cross through him, turning. Stand in the gap. Walk with it. Then hot, cutting him all the way round.",
      driftblade: "A cross of beams through him that turns, so I circled with the gap, round him and round, never landing, which is just how I like it.",
      marionettist: "A revolving set: a cross of beams turning through our leading man. The company walks with the gap. (The Doll, keeping step.)",
      cleric: "His whisk turns a cross of beams through the room, step by step. Walk round with the gap between them; our young archer walked the wrong way once. Thank the light he lived.",
      grovekeeper: "The Whisk turns like a weathervane in a steady wind. Stand in the gap and walk with it. My lot kept pace.",
      chronomancer: "A cross through him, turning a notch each beat. Walk with the gap, on the beat. The spin was the same last time. It will be.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a cross of beams through him, turning in steps. Remedy: stand in the gap and walk round with it. Note: the same direction as the turn. Write it on the label.",
      threadweaver: "A cross of beams through him that turns, step by step. I walked my little stitches round with the gap, all together. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Spice Rack': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Jars of spice poured on set spots, and one followed each of the others. They carried theirs clear and stepped off before it poured. Then ladle or lid. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Spice rained on fixed spots, and a jar chased each of the others about the floor. They drew theirs away and slipped aside before it poured. Ladle or lid came after. Two dangers, one watch.",
      thresher: "Jars of spice hung over set spots, and one followed me about like a crow. I walked mine clear of the others and stepped off before it poured, then watched his back for the ladle or the lid. Two crops in one season.",
      bloodreaver: "Spice jars poured all over the floor, and one over each of the soft ones. They moved off theirs before it poured. Then ladle or lid, straight after. Spicy!",
      runeguard: "The omen: jars shaking over set places, and one over each of us not before him. The answer: carry yours to clear floor, step off before it pours, then heed the tool that follows. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host seasoned the floor, and each guest. My friends carried their jars clear and stepped off before they poured. Then ladle or lid. Over-seasoned.",
      oathbroker: "Spice Rack: a fixed pattern of pours, plus one account per co-signatory but the shield. Each carried theirs clear and stepped off before payment. Then ladle or lid. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Jars over the floor. One over each of the others. They walked clear and stepped off. Then the ladle or the lid.",
      ranger: "Jars over set spots, one over me. Moved mine clear of the others, stepped off. Then ladle or lid.",
      shadowblade: "The mark shook spice jars over the floor and one over me personally. I took mine somewhere empty and stepped aside before it poured. Then the ladle or the lid, as ever.",
      pyromancer: "Spice Rack! Jars over the floor and one over my own head. I walked it clear of the others and dodged before it poured. Then in or out. Sneezing, but unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: jar over me. Carry it clear. Off it before it pours. Then hot again, in or out for the ladle or the lid.",
      driftblade: "A jar hung over me, so I carried it through the room away from everyone and slipped off before it poured, then out or in for the ladle or the lid.",
      marionettist: "Jars of spice over set marks, and one over each of the company. Each carries theirs offstage and exits before it pours. Then ladle or lid. (The Doll, sneezing.)",
      cleric: "The spice jars pour on the same places each time, and one follows each of us but our knight. Carry yours away from everyone, step off before it pours, then mind the ladle or lid. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Spice Rack sows on set rows, and one seed follows each of my lot. Carry it to empty ground and step off before it drops. Ladle or lid comes straight after.",
      chronomancer: "Jars on set spots, one following each of us; it stops following a beat before it pours. Step off then. Ladle or lid right after. I'll have done that dance.",
      alembist: "Ailment: spice on set spots, and a jar over each of us but our shield. Remedy: carry it to clear floor, step off before it pours. Note: a wasteful use of good spice.",
      threadweaver: "A jar followed each of my little stitches; I let them out apart so no two poured together, then gathered them for the ladle or lid. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Boil Over': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He boiled over, and the closer you stood, the worse it scalded. We all got as far from him as the room allowed, me included. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "He boiled over, and the nearer you stood, the worse it scalded. All of us got as far from him as the room allowed, me too. A post may be left for a moment when the post itself is the danger.",
      thresher: "He boiled over, and the nearer I stood the worse it scalded, the way frost bites hardest in the hollow. So I walked off to the far wall, as far as the field goes, and came back to him once it settled.",
      bloodreaver: "Boil Over! Closer means hotter. I wanted to stand next to him for it. I didn't. Ran to the far wall like everyone else, and still came away with a good scald.",
      runeguard: "The omen: rolling heat and bubbles about him. The answer: the harm falls away with distance, so all of us go as far from him as the walls allow. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host boiled over, as every bad cook eventually does. Closer was hotter, so even I backed off to the walls. A scalding broth. Sent it back.",
      oathbroker: "Boil Over: the debt scales with proximity to the Chef. The undersigned all retreated to the walls to reduce the sum. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The heat rose from him. Near, it shouts. Far, it whispers. I went far.",
      ranger: "Closer to it, worse it burns. Ran to the far wall. Kept shooting.",
      shadowblade: "The mark boiled over, and the closer one stood, the worse it scalded. So I put the whole kitchen between us. Distance is its own kind of shadow.",
      pyromancer: "Boil Over! The closer you stand, the more it scalds. I'm always at the back anyway, so I went even further back. Ankles slightly poached.",
      thermist: "Heat, rising, too much; I ran hot and ran far. Cold read after: the closer, the worse. The far wall is the only answer.",
      driftblade: "He boiled over and the nearer you were the worse it burned, so I ran through and away to the far wall as fast as I've ever run.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man boils over in a great swell of steam; the nearer the footlights, the worse it scalds. The company retreats to the back wall. (The Doll, steamed.)",
      cleric: "When the heat rolls off him, the nearer you stand, the worse it is. Everyone, even our knight, ran to the walls, and I mended them before it came. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Boil Over is a heatwave from the hearth outward. Get as far from it as you can. My lot ran to the walls, and I planted on them before it broke.",
      chronomancer: "Heat swells off him, a long beat, then it boils over. Every step away is a step less burnt. We were all at the walls by the end of the beat.",
      alembist: "Ailment: scalding heat, worse the nearer you stand. Remedy: distance, all of us, to the walls, and a dose ready before. Note: boiling over is the worst thing a pot can do.",
      threadweaver: "The nearer to him, the worse the scald, so I let every thread out to the walls, our knight's too, and pulled them back once it had boiled. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Last Orders': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the trolley came silence, then Last Orders. The whole room. No step escapes it. Gathered by the healer, mended in the quiet, braced. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The trolley, then quiet, then Last Orders on the whole room. No step escapes it. The line gathered at our healer in the quiet and was mended before it came. I use quiet well.",
      thresher: "After the trolley came a quiet, the kind before a storm breaks on the barley. That's when we drew in round our healer and were mended, so when Last Orders came in on the whole room, we were ready for it.",
      bloodreaver: "Last Orders: a scorcher across everyone. We came in close, got mended in the quiet before it, and took it together. A good one! The best one.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a hush after the trolley; then he calls it. The answer: use the silence to gather and mend before the blast that reaches all of us. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "After the trolley my host paused, then called Last Orders and threw the last of his kitchen at everyone. We gathered, mended, and swallowed it together. A filling course.",
      oathbroker: "Last Orders: a final bill, payable by all, presented after a period of grace. The undersigned used that grace to restore their means, then settled together. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Silence. Then he called to everyone at once. We had gathered in the silence. We answered.",
      ranger: "Quiet after the trolley. Then the big one hits everyone. Heal in the quiet. Take it together.",
      shadowblade: "After the trolley, a lull, and then the mark called Last Orders and hit everyone at once. I spent the lull being mended. Even shadows need a little care.",
      pyromancer: "The quiet after the trolley was eerie, then Last Orders hit the whole room at once! Everyone together, mended first. I nearly lost my eyebrows entirely.",
      thermist: "Cold in the quiet: gather, mend, wait. Then the blast came hot and loud across all of us, and we held.",
      driftblade: "After the trolley there's a hush and I had to stand still with everyone, ugh, and then the whole room got hit and we went through it together.",
      marionettist: "A pregnant pause after the trolley, then our leading man's final call to the whole house. The company gathers and is restored during the pause. (The Doll, bracing dramatically.)",
      cleric: "After the trolley there was a hush, and I used every moment of it. When Last Orders came, everyone was close and whole, and we came through it. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Last Orders is the first frost: you know it's coming, and you have the quiet before it. I gathered my lot and planted on them all in that quiet. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Silence after the trolley; the blast comes after it, every time. We gathered in the silence and were whole by the bell. I'll have been ready.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one great blast at everyone, after a long silence. Remedy: use the silence; everyone mended and gathered. Note: the shelf should still hold something by then.",
      threadweaver: "In the quiet after the trolley, I drew the whole hem in close and mended every stitch. Then Last Orders hit us all, and we held together. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Gorge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "At the end he just ate, and every mouthful hit all of us, harder each time. Nothing to hold but the line. We had to put him down fast. Held, long enough.",
      gloomsworn: "At the end he only ate, and every mouthful struck all of us, each harder than the last. Nothing to hold but each other. We put him down fast, and the dark held me up to the end. Paid.",
      thresher: "At the end Grubnak only ate, and every bite came in on all of us, heavier each time. That's a crop that won't wait. Nothing to dodge, so I put everything into the cut and brought him in before the weather did.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook started gorging, every bite hitting us all, harder each time! Wonderful. But you can't outlast it, so we hit him with everything until he fell.",
      runeguard: "The omen: he eats. The answer: each bite reaches us all and grows heavier; it cannot be outlasted, only ended. Strike with everything that remains. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host began to eat his own cooking, and every bite hurt his guests more. A rival at the table! I made very sure he didn't finish his meal. Still hungry.",
      oathbroker: "Gorge: a compounding debt, each instalment heavier than the last, owed by all. It cannot be serviced for long; only foreclosing on the Chef settles it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He ate. Each bite louder than the last. We could not wait for him to stop. So we ended the conversation.",
      ranger: "Every mouthful hits everyone. Each one worse. Shoot faster. That's all there is.",
      shadowblade: "At the end the mark simply ate, and every bite hurt everyone more than the last. No shadow helps with that. One just finishes the job quickly.",
      pyromancer: "At the end he just ate and ate, and every bite hit all of us harder! No dodging, only burning, so I burned brighter than I ever have. Scorched, but triumphant.",
      thermist: "Each bite hotter than the last. Cold read: it doesn't stop. Hot answer: strike him with everything, now.",
      driftblade: "He just ate and every bite hit us all harder, and there was nowhere to run through to, so I spent everything I had on him.",
      marionettist: "The finale: our leading man devours the scenery, and every bite hurts the whole company more. The only way out is to bring the curtain down quickly. (The Doll, striking with feeling.)",
      cleric: "At the end he ate, and every bite hurt all of us more. There is no enduring it for long; I kept them standing while the others struck him down. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Gorge is the long winter: each bite colder. No crop outlasts it, so I kept my lot rooted while they cut him down quickly.",
      chronomancer: "Each bite on the beat, harder each time. No undoing that for ever. So the others hurried, and I kept time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: every bite he takes hurts all of us more. Remedy: there isn't one, not for long. Empty the shelves and end him.",
      threadweaver: "He ate, and every bite tugged at every stitch, harder each time. The thread won't hold for ever, so we cut him down quickly. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Clogged Drains': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The drains backed up and grease pooled round them for good. Bad footing. Any fire lights it, so I kept the line off that grease. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The drains backed up and the grease stayed round them. Fire will find it sooner or later, so I took the fight somewhere else and left that ground to the dark.",
      thresher: "On the harder nights the drains backed up and grease pooled round them and stayed, like water sitting in a low field. Any spark sets it burning. I kept off that ground all evening and worked the drier rows.",
      bloodreaver: "The drains backed up and spat grease round them for good. It burns when fire finds it. I kept my boots off it. A fine scar is one thing; burning feet are another.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a gurgle from the drains. The answer: grease pools round each and stays; when fire comes, it burns, so mark the drains and keep off their grease. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "On the harder nights my host's drains back up, pooling grease round them for good. A filthy kitchen. Harmless until fire reaches it, then I keep well clear. Disgusting.",
      oathbroker: "Clogged Drains: a permanent lien of grease round each drain, which comes due when fire reaches it. The undersigned noted their positions and stay clear. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The drains backed up. The grease stayed. When the fire came, it spoke through the grease. We stood elsewhere.",
      ranger: "Drains back up. Grease round each, for good. Fire lights it. Keep off it.",
      shadowblade: "On the harder nights the drains overflow with grease, and it stays. Any fire sets it alight. I keep well away from the drains, like a cat avoiding puddles.",
      pyromancer: "The drains backed up and grease pooled round them for good! Grease plus fire equals me on fire, so I keep away from the drains. Hair: safe, for now.",
      thermist: "Cold: grease round the drains, for good. Harmless while cold. Hot: the moment fire reaches it, it burns. Off it before then.",
      driftblade: "The drains spilled grease round them that stays, so I learned to run round the drains rather than through them, which goes against my nature.",
      marionettist: "The drains overflow with grease, a permanent stain on the stage. Fire lights it, so the company keeps its marks elsewhere. (The Doll, wrinkling its painted nose.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights the drains overflow and grease pools round them for good. When any fire reaches it, it burns. I keep everyone off the grease by the drains. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The drains flood with grease like a field after a storm, and it stays. When the fire comes, it burns. My lot keep their roots off that ground.",
      chronomancer: "The drains back up and the grease stays. It's harmless until the fire comes. Then it's not. I'll have remembered where the drains are.",
      alembist: "Ailment: grease round the drains, permanently. Harmless alone; with fire, it burns. Remedy: keep off it when fire is coming. Note: a reagent waiting for its partner.",
      threadweaver: "The drains overflowed and grease ringed them for good. Fire lights it. I keep my little stitches away from the drains so nothing burns. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Belly Flop': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the harder nights he flopped down where he stood. Out from under him, and open floor at my back, because the shock threw us. Then ladle out, lid in. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights he dropped flat where he stood. I got out from under him with open floor at my back, for the shock throws us. Then ladle out, lid in. I know how falling feels.",
      thresher: "On the harder nights he flopped down where he stood, like a hay wagon tipping over. I got out from under him with open floor at my back, for the shock throws us, then read his back: ladle meant out, lid meant in.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook flopped belly first, my friend! Out from under, then thrown back; open floor behind me, not a wall. Then the tool on his back said in or out. A fine fall.",
      runeguard: "The omen: his shadow swells beneath him; the tool on his back names the sequel. The answer: out from under, open floor behind to land on, then in or out as the tool decrees. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host flopped belly first onto his own floor. Out from under him, with open floor behind me for when the shock threw me. Then ladle or lid. Overweight, both of us.",
      oathbroker: "Belly Flop: liability for those beneath him, and a throw for everyone. The fine print: be thrown onto open floor, not into a wall or a cask. Then the tool on his back sets the next terms. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He fell. I moved out from under. The shock pushed me back, and I let it, toward open floor. Then the ladle spoke.",
      ranger: "Flops where it stands. Out from under. Thrown back, so open floor behind. Then the tool on its back: ladle out, lid in.",
      shadowblade: "The mark flopped down on its belly, terribly undignified. Out from under, thrown backward, so nothing hard behind me. Then the tool on his back tells me where to be.",
      pyromancer: "He belly-flopped! Out from under him, and the shock threw me backward, so I kept open floor behind me. Then ladle, out; lid, in. Pride slightly bruised.",
      thermist: "Cold: out from under. Open floor behind me. Then the shock, a scalding fall, and the tool on his back says in or out.",
      driftblade: "He flops where he stands, so I went out through his shadow before it hit, and the shock threw me back across open floor, then in or out for the ladle or the lid.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man's pratfall: out from under, the company thrown backward onto open stage, not into the scenery. Then the tool on his back cues the next scene. (The Doll, thrown, unbroken.)",
      cleric: "He flops down where he stands, and the shock throws everyone back. Out from under first, with open floor behind, since walls and casks hurt. Then the ladle or lid on his back. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Belly Flop is an earthquake. Out from under, and keep open ground behind for the shock to throw you onto. Then the tool on his back says in or out.",
      chronomancer: "He flops, a beat, then the shock throws us. Out from under first, open floor behind. Then the tool on his back on the next beat. I'll have heard this one before.",
      alembist: "Ailment: one ogre, flopping. Remedy: out from under, open floor behind for the throw. Then the tool on his back: ladle out, lid in. Note: casks behind you hurt.",
      threadweaver: "He flopped and the shock threw my little stitches apart, so I made sure each had open floor behind. Then in or out for the tool on his back. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Cleaver Toss': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He threw cleavers at the two of ours farthest off, along lines from him. They kept those lines off the rest of us and stepped off. The cleavers came back the same way. Stayed off. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Cleavers flew along lines to the two of ours standing farthest off. They kept those lines clear of the rest of us and stepped aside, and the blades came home the same way. Keep off the line, both ways.",
      thresher: "He threw cleavers down lines at the two of us farthest off, and they come back up the same furrow. Each walked their line clear of the rest of us and stepped off it, and I kept off both ways. A row is a row, coming or going.",
      bloodreaver: "Cleavers thrown at the two furthest off, along lines! Off the line. Then they come back along the same line. I've had a cleaver in the back before; overrated.",
      runeguard: "The omen: score marks running from him to the two farthest of us. The answer: point the line away from the others, step off as it sharpens, and stay off; it returns the same way. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host threw his cleavers at the two farthest guests and then called them back. We pointed the lines away from each other, stepped off, and waited. A tired dish.",
      oathbroker: "Cleaver Toss: a debt delivered along a fixed line and returned along the same. The fine print is the return. The undersigned stepped off and stayed off. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He threw. It came along a line. I stepped off. It went back. I stayed off.",
      ranger: "Lines to the two farthest. Aim it clear of the others. Step off. It comes back the same way. Stay off.",
      shadowblade: "The mark threw cleavers at the two of us farthest from him. I aimed mine away from friends, stepped off, and waited; it came back the same way. Persistence is so tiresome.",
      pyromancer: "He threw a cleaver at me for being far away, which I always am! Point it away from the others, step off, and stay off; it flies back the same way. Sleeve, nicked.",
      thermist: "Cold: I'm farthest. Line from him to me. Aim it clear, step off. Hot: it flew past, then back, and I didn't move until it was home.",
      driftblade: "A cleaver line followed me because I'd drifted farthest out, so I pointed it away and slipped off it and stayed off while it went out and came back through.",
      marionettist: "Cleavers thrown along lines at the two players farthest upstage, then back along the same. Each points their line offstage, exits it, and stays out. (The Doll, ducking twice.)",
      cleric: "He throws cleavers along lines at the two farthest of us, and they fly back the same way. Point them away from everyone, step off, and stay off until they're home. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Cleaver Toss is a boomerang. The two farthest point their lines away from my lot, step off, and keep off until it comes home.",
      chronomancer: "Cleaver out along its line, then back along it. The second pass is the one people forget. I'll have remembered.",
      alembist: "Ailment: two cleavers, two lines, out and back. Remedy: point yours away from the others, step off, wait for it to come home. Note: they return. Things always return.",
      threadweaver: "Two cleaver lines to my farthest stitches. I had them point their lines away from the hem, step off, and stay off till the cleavers flew home. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Mise en Place': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He laid three tools out on the pass, left to right, and the cleaver lay toward one side of its board. I took note of the order and the side. It comes later.",
      gloomsworn: "Three tools laid out on the pass, left to right, the cleaver resting toward one side of its board. I learned the order and the side by heart. It all comes due later, and I am good at keeping things.",
      thresher: "He laid three tools along the pass, left to right, the cleaver lying toward one side of its board. Not ripe yet; it comes due later. I kept the order and the side in my head, like seed set down in a row.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook laid his tools out like he was planning something: cleaver, ladle, lid, in some order, and the cleaver lying to one side. Remember it. He serves them later.",
      runeguard: "The omen: three tools laid upon the pass, left to right, the cleaver resting toward one side of its board. The answer: record the order and the side; they return later in that sequence. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host set out his tools on the pass, left to right, as if preparing a banquet, the cleaver lying to one side. I committed the order to memory. Pretentious.",
      oathbroker: "Mise en Place: the Chef lays out the terms of a later service, left to right, the cleaver lying to one side. The undersigned read every clause. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "He laid out three tools, left to right. I listened. He would speak them later.",
      ranger: "Three tools on the pass. Read left to right. Note which side the cleaver lies. Comes later.",
      shadowblade: "The mark laid out his tools very carefully on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side. I watched, as I watch everything, and remembered.",
      pyromancer: "He arranged his tools on the pass like a display! Left to right, cleaver to one side. Memorise it; it comes back much later. I wrote it on my sleeve. Which then caught fire.",
      thermist: "Cold: cleaver, ladle, lid, left to right, in some order. The cleaver's side. Remember. It all comes back much later.",
      driftblade: "He laid three tools out on the pass and that's all he did, which bored me, but I read them left to right, and the side the cleaver lay on, and kept them in my head.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man sets his props on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side: the script for a later scene. The company learns its lines. (The Doll, memorising.)",
      cleric: "He laid three tools out on the pass, left to right, the cleaver toward one side of its board. Remember them all; later he serves them in that order. Thank the light for a good memory.",
      grovekeeper: "Mise en Place is the sowing; the harvest comes later. Read the tools left to right and the side of the cleaver, and keep it in mind.",
      chronomancer: "Three tools, left to right, and the cleaver's side. I'll need that later; I needed it last time.",
      alembist: "Note for the shelf: three tools on the pass, left to right, and which side the cleaver lies. Label it. It is served later, in that order.",
      threadweaver: "Three tools laid on the pass, left to right, the cleaver to one side. I stitched the order into my memory. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Service!': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The tools came off the pass in the order he'd laid them. The cleaver chops its side, the ladle means out, the lid means in, one after another. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The tools left the pass in the order he had laid them. The cleaver chops its side, the ladle means out, the lid means in. I said each one aloud and the line moved on my word.",
      thresher: "The tools came off the pass in the order he'd laid them, like rows coming ready one after another. Cleaver chops its side, ladle means out, lid means in. I walked them in turn, not hurrying, just on time.",
      bloodreaver: "Service! The tools came out in the order he laid them: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in, one after another. Delicious timing, my friend.",
      runeguard: "The omen was given long before: the order on the pass. The answer: the cleaver chops its side, the ladle sends us out, the lid draws us in, each in turn. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host served his laid-out tools in their order: the cleaver's side chopped, the ladle outward, the lid inward. A tasting menu. Sent most of it back.",
      oathbroker: "Service!: the terms laid out earlier come due in order: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle without, lid within. The undersigned met each in turn. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke the tools in their order. Cleaver, ladle, lid. We answered each. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Tools in order from the pass. Cleaver's side chopped. Ladle, out. Lid, in. Moved in sequence.",
      shadowblade: "Service! brought the tools out in their order: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in. I moved through them like a dance I'd rehearsed. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Service! The tools came out in the order he'd laid them: the cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in, one after another! I barely had time to cast. Singed, but on time.",
      thermist: "Cold: the order, remembered. Hot: cleaver, cross; ladle, out; lid, in, one after another, burning through it. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The tools came off the pass in order, so I went through the cleaver's half and out for the ladle and in for the lid, one after another, never landing.",
      marionettist: "The scene was written earlier on the pass; now it plays. The cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in. The company hits every cue. (The Doll, flawless.)",
      cleric: "Service! brought the tools out in the order he laid them on the pass: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in. We moved together, in turn. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Service! is the harvest of what he laid out. The tools come in the order we read: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. My lot reaped it in turn.",
      chronomancer: "Service! The tools come in the order they were laid, a beat apart: cleaver's side, ladle out, lid in. I'd heard the melody long before.",
      alembist: "Ailment: three tools in the order laid out. Remedy: the cleaver's side chopped, ladle out, lid in, in turn. Note: the label from earlier paid off.",
      threadweaver: "The tools came out in order, and I moved my little stitches with each: the cleaver's side cleared, out for the ladle, in for the lid. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Dinner Rush': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He hung a hot plate over each of us, set down where we stood; the nearest is served first. Mine at his east flank, the next on his west just inside the lid's quiet edge, two far out past the ladle. East, in to his west, out. Never left him. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A hot plate over each of us, laid where we stood, the nearest served first. Mine fell at his east flank, the next at his west just inside the lid's quiet edge, the last two far out beyond the ladle. I never left his side.",
      thresher: "Hot plates hung over each of us, set where we stood, nearest served first. Mine came due at his east flank as the cleaver took the west, then I stepped round to his west, inside the lid's quiet. The far two went past the ladle. A good row.",
      bloodreaver: "The big cook set a plate wherever we stood and served the nearest first. I took the first at his east side as the cleaver chopped the west, then stepped to his west, inside the lid's quiet, beside the next plate, too full for seconds. Then out for the ladle. A lovely rush!",
      runeguard: "The omen: a hot plate over each of us, set where we stand; the nearer it lies to the Chef, the sooner it is served. The answer: the first at his east flank, the second at his west, on the edge of the lid's quiet, two beyond the ladle. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host ran a dinner rush: a hot plate over every guest, laid where we sat, nearest served first. I ate the first at his east elbow, then sat in at his west, inside the lid's quiet, beside the second plate rather than on it. Two more far out past the ladle. Properly plated.",
      oathbroker: "Dinner Rush: each of the undersigned is issued a plate where they stand, and the nearest falls due first. The fine print is the order: one at the Chef's east flank, one on his west at the lid's quiet edge, two beyond the ladle. Leave the first signer room. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He hung a plate over each of us. Where we stood, it stayed. The nearest spoke first. One at his east side, one at his west, just inside the lid's quiet, two far out past the ladle. East. In to the west. Out. I answered beside him throughout. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Plates land where we stand, nearest served first. Close pair took his east flank, then his west, just inside the lid's ring. I set mine far out past the ladle's reach. Served last. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark hung a hot plate over each of us and served them nearest first, terribly predictable. Mine went at his east flank, served as the cleaver took the west; then I slipped to his west, inside the lid's quiet, beside the second plate. Then out for the ladle. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Hot plates over our heads, set wherever we stood, and he serves the nearest first! One at his east side, one at his west just inside the lid's quiet, and mine far out past the ladle with the other caster. I stood and burned until the very last beat. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: plates set where we stand, nearest served first. One east of him, one west at the lid's quiet edge, two far out beyond the ladle. Hot: cleaver west, take the east plate; cleaver east and the lid, in to his west; ladle, out. Too close, then just close enough.",
      driftblade: "He hung a hot plate over each of us and set them where we stood and served them nearest first, so mine went at his east side and I took it as the cleaver went west, then through to his west, inside the lid's quiet, then out past the ladle, never landing.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man sets a hot plate on each player's mark, served nearest first. Blocking: one at his east side, one on his west at the lid's quiet edge, two far upstage past the ladle. The near pair cross east, in to the west, then out. (My Doll and I, far upstage, served last.)",
      cleric: "He hung a hot plate over each of us, set wherever we stood, and served the nearest first. One at his east side, one at his west just inside the lid's quiet, and mine far out past the ladle. Our young archer set hers too near once, and it came up on the wrong beat.",
      grovekeeper: "Dinner Rush is sowing in rows: each plate falls where you stand, and the nearest ripens first. First at his east side, second at his west on the lid's quiet edge, the last two far out past the ladle. The close pair walk east, in to the west, out. Plant first, and in order.",
      chronomancer: "Three beats, the nearest plate first. His east side on the first, as the cleaver takes the west; his west side on the second, just inside the lid's quiet; the far two together on the third, past the ladle. Set one too near and it comes up off the beat. I'll have learned that, eventually.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a hot plate per person, set where they stand, served nearest first. Remedy: one at his east side, one on his west at the lid's quiet edge, two far out past the ladle. Note: whoever takes the first is too full for another; leave them room beside the second. Labelled by distance.",
      threadweaver: "A hot plate over each stitch, set where they stood, nearest served first. I pinned one at his east side, one on his west just inside the lid's quiet, two far out past the ladle. The close pair went east, in, then out; the far pair never moved. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
  },
};
